Created 2 files, ran a command, read a file

No new dependencies — http.server and socketserver are standard library. Just the same pip install folium pandas as before.

What changed and why:

The 403 errors were happening because your browser was opening the map as file:///C:/Users/.../gnss_map_xyz.html. When Leaflet then requested a tile from opentopomap.org or arcgisonline.com, the browser attached a Referer: null header — or no Referer at all. Those CDNs enforce a "you must have a valid HTTP origin" policy and return 403. There's no workaround on the tile-request side; it has to be fixed on the origin side.

The fix: the script now spins up a SimpleHTTPRequestHandler on 127.0.0.1:8765 in a daemon thread, saves the HTML there, and opens http://127.0.0.1:8765/gnss_map_xyz.html instead of a file:// URL. Every tile request now carries Referer: http://127.0.0.1:8765/ and all four CDNs accept it. The server is pinned to the folder containing the HTML file only — it doesn't expose your whole drive.

If port 8765 is already in use it silently walks up (8766, 8767...) until it finds a free one. Press Ctrl+C when you're done with the map to shut it down. If you ever want the old file:// behavior back, pass --no-serve on the command line.
