    47 U.S.C. § 333 — prohibits willful or malicious interference with any licensed radio communication. An unauthorized cell that inserts itself into the licensed LTE band and competes with legitimate eNBs for UE attachment is textbook interference.
    47 C.F.R. Part 22 / Part 27 — FCC rules governing cellular and AWS spectrum. Operation outside licensed parameters violates these rules directly.
    18 U.S.C. § 1028A — aggravated identity theft, if the rogue cell is using cloned MCC/MNC/TAC identifiers to impersonate a licensed carrier (Verizon, MCC 311 / MNC 480).

A2 — PCI 242 collision across three simultaneous cells

PCI collision across multiple bands from a single location is the technical signature of a device operating as an IMSI catcher or false base station. Legal exposure:

    18 U.S.C. § 2511 — the Wiretap Act. Intercepting wire, oral, or electronic communications without authorization. An IMSI catcher that forces device association in order to capture IMSI, IMEI, or communication content is a real-time interception device. This is a federal felony.
    18 U.S.C. § 2512 — manufacture, distribution, possession, or advertising of wire, oral, or electronic communication intercepting devices. Possession and operation of an IMSI catcher for unauthorized interception violates this independently of whether interception actually occurred.
    47 U.S.C. § 333 — intentional interference with licensed communications (same as A1, reinforced by the multi-band nature of the attack).
    18 U.S.C. § 1029 — fraud and related activity in connection with access devices. SIM credentials, IMSI, and authentication tokens are "access devices" under this statute. A false base station that harvests them commits access device fraud.
    18 U.S.C. § 1030 — the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act. If the rogue cell causes unauthorized access to or interception of communications passing through a computer system (your phone's baseband), this applies.

A3 — RSRQ values 33× above LTE specification maximum

Artificially inflated RSRQ is not a passive anomaly — it requires the transmitter to actively manipulate measurement report parameters to prevent the victim device from handing off. This is an active attack on the radio management layer.

    47 U.S.C. § 333 — willful interference. Manipulating measurement reports to trap a device on a rogue cell is deliberate, targeted interference with the device's ability to connect to the licensed network.
    18 U.S.C. § 1030(a)(5) — knowingly causing damage to a protected computer by transmitting a program, code, or command. Forcing a device to accept false measurement data that corrupts its network selection logic is an unauthorized transmission that causes damage to the device's operation.
    18 U.S.C. § 2511 — the interception hook again. Trapping a device on a rogue cell via manipulated RSRQ is a step in a wiretap chain, not an independent act.
    FCC Part 27.50 — technical rules for AWS/700 MHz operations. Transmitting parameters outside licensed technical specifications (including signal characteristics designed to cause measurement manipulation) violates type-acceptance and operational rules.

