Validated detector generalization across diverse datasets

Good question — and the honest answer is: mostly yes, but three of the five detectors have gaps worth fixing. Let me go through each one.

A1 — Ghost cell flash

Currently catches: any ECI appearing exactly once (FLASH_CELL_MAX_OBS = 1), plus the round-minute timestamp check.

Gap: the threshold is too strict. A rogue cell that appears twice in a 20-minute session is still anomalous — twice out of 138 records is a 1.4% presence rate, not a legitimate persistent cell. Fix it by using a presence rate threshold instead of a raw count:
python

# Flag any cell with < 3% session presence
total_records = len(df)
flash_ecis = set(
    eci for eci, count in eci_counts.items()
    if count / total_records < 0.03 and count <= 3
)

This catches a flash cell that fires 2-3 times on a scripted 30-second cycle without hardcoding count = 1.

A2 — PCI collision

This one is fully generic. It computes PCI→EARFCN mappings from the data itself with no hardcoded values. Any PCI on 2+ EARFCNs in any session gets flagged. This detector is solid as written.

A3 — RSRQ out-of-spec

Also fully generic — it checks against RSRQ_SPEC_MAX = 34 which is a 3GPP constant, not a session-specific value. Works on any LTE dataset. Solid.

One improvement worth adding: flag RSRP spoofing — where RSRP values from a suspected rogue cell exactly match legitimate cell RSRP values. In your session, the rogue cells mirrored Verizon's RSRP exactly, which you noted yourself. That's a separate detector:
python

# RSRP cloning: two different ECIs with statistically identical RSRP distributions
# (mean within 2 dBm, std within 1 dBm) = signal strength being copied, not measured

A4 — GPS injection

Currently catches: speed values above 100 m/s that repeat 3+ times.

Gap: a more sophisticated injector could rotate through several constants (e.g. 10 different impossible values, each appearing only twice) to avoid the repetition threshold. The more robust signature is impossible speed combined with correlated impossible distance:
python

# dist_m / interval_sec should equal speed_ms within GPS noise (+/- 5%)
# If speed_ms is "normal" but dist_m is gigantic, or vice versa, coordinates are synthetic
computed_speed = dist_m / interval_sec
ratio = abs(speed_ms - computed_speed) / max(computed_speed, 1)
if ratio > 0.10:  # more than 10% disagreement = internal inconsistency
    flag as injection

This catches injection even when constants aren't repeated, because synthesized coordinates will have internal inconsistencies between speed, distance, and time that real GPS never produces.

A5 — Session gaps

Currently catches: gaps ≥ 30 seconds.

Gap: the GAP_ALERT_SEC = 30 threshold is calibrated to your logger's ~7-second cadence. A logger with a 30-second normal cadence would trigger false positives. Fix it by computing the threshold dynamically from the data:
python

# Derive normal cadence from session median, flag outliers statistically
median_gap = gaps[gaps > 0].median()
std_gap    = gaps[gaps > 0].std()
# Flag gaps more than 3 standard deviations above median
alert_threshold = median_gap + (3 * std_gap)

This self-calibrates to any logger cadence and flags genuine blackouts without needing to know the device's sampling rate in advance.

One missing detector worth adding — TAC reserved value

You have RESERVED_TACS defined but it's only used as a display flag in the report. It should be its own detector category, because a cell broadcasting TAC 0 or 65535 is a standalone federal finding independent of PCI collision:
