The reason it failed: Python doesn't have true block scope — variables defined inside an if/else are accessible after the block, but only if that branch actually ran. If the else branch somehow didn't fully execute (or if there was a subtle path where the assignment was skipped), the name simply doesn't exist yet when called later. Initializing at the top of the function guarantees all three names exist in main()'s local scope from the moment the function enters, and the loading/splitting block overwrites them when incidents.csv is found. The NLP/GPS split still works exactly the same — you saw NLP incidents: 139  GPS incidents: 1362 print correctly, meaning that code ran fine. The crash was purely the later reference hitting an uninitialized name on a path we didn't fully anticipate.
